]}, "text": "Then the giant seizes two of the men and eats them for his supper.\n\nAfterwards he falls asleep among his sheep.\n\nOdysseus stands over him with his sword half drawn, and for a moment it seems the story is over.\n\nThen he thinks of the stone, and of who would move it, so he waits for the morning.\n\nHe lies awake and counts the men who are left, and the number keeps him awake.\n\nAt breakfast, two more men go, and Homer gives the details, but we will spare you.\n\nThen the giant drives his flock out to pasture, and Odysseus gets to work.\n\nHe finds the giant's own club, an olive trunk as big as a ship's mast.\n\nHe cuts off a length, sharpens it to a point, and hardens the point in the fire.\n\nFor the first time since they entered the cave, the crew have something to do.\n\nThen he hides it under the dung, the one thing a cave full of sheep has plenty of.\n\nFour men are chosen by lot to help him carry it.\n\nThat evening the giant comes home and takes two more.\n\nNow six of the twelve are gone.\n\nEach meal makes the circle of survivors smaller, and the cave a little quieter."}